## Negotiated client-side window chrome

On Linux the app requests client decorations, then branches exclusively on
`Window::window_decorations()`. `DecorationMode::negotiated` is the testable
policy boundary. Never infer decoration mode from `XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP`,
session type, or compositor names; server-decorated windows must not receive a
second title bar or changed workspace bounds.

`window_chrome.rs` centralizes title-bar controls, hit-test roles, frame
metrics, tiling-aware free edges/corners, and resize hit regions. Preserve
these interaction rules:

- compositor-reported capabilities determine which native controls render;
- drag, double-click maximize, and native context menu live on empty title-bar
  space, while controls stop propagation into that region;
- resize hit regions exist only on untiled free edges/corners and sit behind
  application controls;
- fullscreen removes client chrome; maximized/tiled state removes affected
  inset, radius, border, shadow, and resize geometry;
- window-control focus handles stay outside the workspace F6/tab cycle but
  accept pointer focus and Enter/Space activation;
- left/right control placement comes from persisted `[ui]` configuration and
  uses interface-scale geometry.

GNOME Wayland and a server-decorated Linux session are the validated Linux
configurations. GNOME X11 is outside the supported configuration set.

